High Performance Computing
Focus areas, in order of priority
1. Why HPC exists: dividing one large problem across many processors so that it finishes in far less wall clock time.
2. OpenMP versus MPI: which one works across the cores of a single shared memory node and which one passes messages between nodes of a distributed memory cluster.
3. Amdahl’s law: what limits the maximum speedup, and how to compute it.
4. Job schedulers such as SLURM: why users submit jobs rather than running programs directly on compute nodes.
This course is assessed at beginner level through recognition and terminology. The practical material below goes a little beyond what is required, because the concepts are much easier to remember once you have seen a few lines of real code.

What HPC is and why it exists
High performance computing means aggregating many processors, usually many whole computers, to work on a single problem at once. The purpose is time to solution: a weather forecast that takes thirty hours on one machine is useless, and the same forecast split across two thousand cores and finished in one hour is a product. Genome assembly, fluid dynamics, structural simulation, financial risk modelling, training large neural networks, and mining fraud patterns across years of transaction data are all HPC workloads for the same reason.
Three things HPC is not. It is not about storing more data on a single disk (that is a storage problem; HDFS and object stores address it). It is not about reducing electricity use (HPC facilities are among the largest power consumers in computing). It is not an operating system or a replacement for a desktop workstation.

Parallel computer architectures
Shared memory. One machine with several cores that all see the same memory. A modern server with two processor sockets and 64 cores is a shared memory system. Any core can read what another core wrote, so parallel work is coordinated through shared variables. The limit is physical: one machine only holds so many cores and so much memory.
Distributed memory. Many machines (nodes), each with its own processors and its own memory, connected by a fast network (the interconnect, typically InfiniBand or high speed Ethernet). A core on node 5 cannot read the memory of node 9; the two must exchange explicit messages. This is how a cluster scales to thousands of nodes.
Hybrid. In practice every cluster is both: distributed memory between nodes, shared memory within each node. Programs are often written to match, with MPI between nodes and OpenMP within them.
Accelerators. GPUs attach to a node and offer thousands of simple cores suited to data parallel work such as matrix multiplication, which is why deep learning runs on them. They are programmed with CUDA, HIP, OpenCL or through libraries such as TensorFlow and PyTorch that hide the details.

Anatomy of a cluster
Component
Role
Login (head) node
Where users connect, edit, compile, and submit jobs. Not for running heavy work
Compute nodes
Where jobs execute. Users reach them only through the scheduler
Interconnect
Low latency, high bandwidth network between nodes (InfiniBand)
Parallel file system
Shared storage visible from every node (Lustre, GPFS, BeeGFS)
Job scheduler
Software that queues jobs and allocates nodes (SLURM, PBS, LSF)
Module system
Manages software versions (module load gcc/13 openmpi/4)
Programming models: OpenMP and MPI
OpenMP
OpenMP parallelises work across the cores of a single shared memory node. The programmer adds compiler directives (#pragma omp in C and C++, !$omp in Fortran) to existing loops and the compiler creates a team of threads that divide the iterations among themselves. It follows a fork join model: the program runs as one thread, forks into many at a parallel region, and joins back into one at the end. Because all threads share memory, there is no data movement to write, but shared variables must be protected against simultaneous updates.
#include <stdio.h>
#include <omp.h>

int main(void) {
    long n = 100000000;
    double sum = 0.0;

    /* Iterations are split among threads; reduction combines the partial sums */
    #pragma omp parallel for reduction(+:sum)
    for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum += 1.0 / (i + 1);
    }

    printf("threads: %d  sum: %f\n", omp_get_max_threads(), sum);
    return 0;
}
Compile with gcc -fopenmp harmonic.c -o harmonic and run with OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 ./harmonic. Without reduction(+:sum) the threads would race to update sum and the result would be wrong; that race condition is the classic shared memory bug.

MPI
MPI (the Message Passing Interface) is the standard for distributed memory programming: processes that run on different nodes, each with private memory, and that communicate by sending and receiving messages over the interconnect. MPI is a specification with several implementations (Open MPI, MPICH, Intel MPI). A program is started as many copies at once with mpirun -np 64 ./program; each copy learns its rank (0 to 63) and the total count, and decides from its rank which part of the work to do. Data is exchanged with calls such as MPI_Send, MPI_Recv, MPI_Bcast, MPI_Scatter, MPI_Gather and MPI_Reduce.
#include <stdio.h>
#include <mpi.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    MPI_Init(&argc, &argv);
    int rank, size;
    MPI_Comm_rank(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &rank);   /* which process am I */
    MPI_Comm_size(MPI_COMM_WORLD, &size);   /* how many processes in total */
    printf("process %d of %d\n", rank, size);
    MPI_Finalize();
    return 0;
}
Compile with mpicc hello.c -o hello and run with mpirun -np 4 ./hello. The same program in Python with mpi4py:
from mpi4py import MPI

comm = MPI.COMM_WORLD
rank = comm.Get_rank()
size = comm.Get_size()

local = sum(range(rank, 1_000_000, size))      # each process sums a strided slice
total = comm.reduce(local, op=MPI.SUM, root=0) # combine the partial sums on rank 0

if rank == 0:
    print(f"{size} processes, total = {total}")
Run with mpirun -np 4 python sum.py.

