The examination asks for the home territory of each: OpenMP is for the cores of a single shared memory node; MPI is for exchanging messages between processes on different nodes of a distributed memory cluster. Neither has anything to do with remote databases or geographically separate clusters.

Measuring parallel performance
Speedup on N processors is S(N) = T(1) / T(N), the serial run time divided by the parallel run time. Efficiency is S(N) / N; perfect efficiency of 1.0 means every processor is fully productive, which never happens at scale.

Amdahl’s law
Every program has a fraction that cannot be parallelised: reading input, setting up, a serial dependency, writing results. Call the parallelisable fraction P and the serial fraction 1 - P. On N processors the parallel part shrinks by a factor of N but the serial part does not change, so:
S(N) = 1 / ( (1 - P) + P / N )
As N grows without limit, P / N goes to zero and the speedup approaches 1 / (1 - P). That ceiling depends only on the serial fraction, not on how many cores you buy.
