A worked example: a job is 90 percent parallelisable and runs on 10 cores. S = 1 / (0.1 + 0.9 / 10) = 1 / 0.19 = 5.26. Doubling to 20 cores gives 1 / (0.1 + 0.045) = 6.90. A thousand cores give 9.91. The job can never run more than ten times faster, because one tenth of it runs on a single core no matter what. The practical lesson is that shrinking the serial fraction (faster input handling, less setup) often pays more than adding hardware.
The question asks what limits maximum speedup: the serial fraction. Not the core count (more cores help only up to the ceiling), not the dataset size, not the clock speed.
Gustafson’s law gives the complementary view: if you keep the run time fixed and grow the problem as you add cores, the serial fraction shrinks relative to the whole, and speedup can scale nearly linearly. This is why large clusters are used for larger problems rather than for finishing small problems faster. The two views are called strong scaling (fixed problem, more cores, governed by Amdahl) and weak scaling (problem grows with cores, governed by Gustafson).
Real speedups fall short of both laws because of communication time between processes, load imbalance (some processes finish early and wait), and contention for memory and file system bandwidth.

Job schedulers
A cluster is shared by many users, and compute nodes must not be fought over. Users therefore never run programs directly on compute nodes. They write a small batch script describing the resources they need (nodes, cores, memory, time limit) and submit it to a job scheduler, also called a workload manager or resource manager. The scheduler queues the job, waits until the requested resources are free, allocates them exclusively, starts the job, and releases the nodes when it ends or exceeds its time limit. It enforces fairness, priorities and accounting along the way. SLURM is the most common scheduler on modern clusters; PBS Professional, Torque and LSF are alternatives.

A SLURM batch script
#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH --job-name=txn-risk           # name shown in the queue
#SBATCH --partition=compute           # the queue to submit to
#SBATCH --nodes=2                     # number of nodes
#SBATCH --ntasks-per-node=16          # MPI processes per node
#SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1             # OpenMP threads per process
#SBATCH --mem=32G                     # memory per node
#SBATCH --time=01:30:00               # wall clock limit, hh:mm:ss
#SBATCH --output=txn-risk_%j.out      # %j expands to the job ID

module load gcc/13 openmpi/4
export OMP_NUM_THREADS=$SLURM_CPUS_PER_TASK

srun ./risk_model input.dat            # srun launches the 32 MPI processes
Everyday commands
Command
Purpose
sbatch job.sh
Submit a batch script; prints the job ID
squeue -u $USER
Show your queued and running jobs and their state (PD pending, R running)
scancel 12345
Cancel a job
sinfo
Show partitions and node availability
srun --pty bash
Request an interactive session on a compute node
sacct -j 12345
Accounting record of a finished job: run time, memory used, exit code
scontrol show job 12345
Full details of a job
A job that requests more time or memory than it needs waits longer in the queue; a job that requests less than it needs is killed when it crosses the limit. Good estimates come from running a small test case first and reading sacct.

Self-check questions
1. A department wants its quarterly risk simulation, which currently takes 40 hours, to finish overnight. Which technology addresses this, and what is the underlying idea?
2. You have one server with 32 cores and a loop that dominates run time. Which programming model fits, and what directive would you add?
3. You have 50 nodes and the data does not fit in one node’s memory. Which programming model is required, and why can OpenMP alone not solve it?
4. A program is 80 percent parallelisable. Compute its speedup on 4, 16 and 1,000 cores, and state the ceiling.
5. Why are users forbidden from running programs directly on compute nodes, and what do they do instead?
6. Name the SLURM commands to submit a job, list your jobs, and cancel a job.
7. What is a race condition, and which OpenMP clause prevented one in the harmonic sum example?
